ources, but we can use FFT-based convolution to understand the peaks).\n\ne) Spectral Shaping:\n   - Create a low-pass spectral version of the sequence to damp high frequency components.\n\nf) Time-Domain Shaping:\n   - Refine time-domain sequence properties by clamping and scaling.\n\ng) Final Validation:\n   - After the iteration, validate the best candidate and return it.\n\nThe security checks will be applied during objective evaluation.  \nThe function `evaluate_sequence` is used as the gold standard to compute the objective.\n\nWe use the following helper functions:\n\n```python\ndef evaluate_normalized(sequence: list[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Evaluate normalized sequence loss.\"\"\"\n    n = len(sequence)\n    if n == 0:\n        return np.inf\n    sum_val = sum(sequence)\n    if sum_val < 0.01:\n        return np.inf\n    conv = np.convolve(sequence, sequence, mode='full')\n    max_val = np.max(conv)\n    return 2.0 * n * max_val / sum_val**2\n\ndef gradient_descent_step(sequence, alpha=0.01):\n    \"\"\"Perform a gradient descent step to reduce peaks, may use FFT to find gradient.\"\"\"\n    conv = np.convolve(sequence, sequence, mode='full')\n    # Pick the convolution indices that have the highest poperties (here, the maximum values)\n    # This is a placeholder, the actual implementation needs to be more detailed.\n    n_effective = len(sequence)  # For the normalized version, length doesn't scale directly\n    a_opt = aggressive_optimize(sequence)  # This needs to be defined\n    return a_opt\n\ndef frequency_shaping(sequence):\n    \"\"\"Create a low-pass filtered version of the sequence.\"\"\"\n    # Placeholder function: where the actual implementation would go\n    pass\n```\n\nWe'll call the above functions in place.\n\nNote: Due to the complexity and time constraints, the gradient descent and spectral shaping steps require detailed implementation which we'll provide in the code.\n\nImplementation Details:  \nThe following plan integrates the above steps and strategies within the 1500 second budget.\n\n1. Initialization:  \n   - Load parent sequence if available (420) and normalize it.\n   - Define candidate lengths `n_values = [400,420,440,480,500]`\n\n2.Core Optimization (for each n in n_values):  \n   - Generate a normalized random sequence if parent is not available.\n   - Apply gradient descent forcing weak points to normalize around the target (if needed).\n   - Target the convolution peak with a step:\n\n```python\ndef propose_candidate(seed=42, budget_s=1500):\n    start_time = time.time()\n    deadline = start_time + budget_s - 30\n    best_candidate = None\n    best_score = np.inf\n    \n    # Load parent if available\n    if height_sequence_1 is not None and len(height_sequence_1) > 0:\n        try:\n            parent = np.asarray(height_sequence_1, dtype=np.float64)\n            parent = [0.0 if x < -1 else 1000.0 if x > 1000 else x for x in parent]\n            if any(x < 0 for x in parent):  # Defensive normalize\n                parent = [x - min(0, x - min(parent)) for x in parent]\n            # Scale for desired sum\n            total = sum(parent)\n            parent = [x / total * 0.98 for x in parent]  # 98% to avoid boundary issues\n            parent = [min(1000, max(0, x)) for x in parent]\n            parent_sum = sum(parent)\n            if parent_sum < 0.01:\n                parent = [x * (1/parent_sum) for x in parent]  # Normalize to avoid inf\n            candidate = parent\n            score = evaluate_sequence(candidate)\n            if score < best_score:\n                best_score = score\n                best_candidate = candidate.copy()\n        except Exception as e:\n            pass\n    \n    if best_candidate is None:\n        seed_for_start = seed\n        candidate_length = len(random_init(400, seed_for_start))\n        candidate = random_init(candidate_length, seed_for_start)\n        best_candidate = candidate.copy()\n        best_score = np.inf\n    \n    n_values = sorted(set([400,420,440,480,500]))  # Consider variations\n    improvement_found = False\n    for n in n_values:\n        if time.time() > deadline - 10:  # Approximate check (fallback to 30s validation)\n            break\n        # Access and get info on current best_candidate\n        if n == 420 and (evaluate_sequence(best_candidate) < best_score or has_sufficient_peaks(best_candidate)):\n            continue  # Let's stick with the parent unless we find a better candidate\n        # If different length, continue to the iteration below\n        time_allocated = min(deadline - time.time(), 200)  # 200 seconds per candidate if there's time, else sooner\n        if abs(n - 420) < 10 and (has_parent_improvement or has_homogeneous_peak_reduction):\n            n_specific = n  # Targeting near parent values\n            a_start = best_candidate.copy()\n            polish_config = optimize_using_polish(a_start, time_budget=time_allocated)\n            a_opt, obj_opt = polish_config[0], polish_config[1]\n            # Now, ensure peaks are down...\n        else:\n            # Random optimization for different length scales\n            pass\n        \n        # Then, evaluate the best candidate\n        score_current = evaluate_sequence(a_opt)\n        if score_current < best_score - 1e-5:\n            best_score = score_current\n            best_candidate = a_opt.copy()\n            improvement_found = True\n        elif score_current < best_score - 1e-3 and gain_sufficient_momentum:\n            best_score = score_current\n            best_candidate = a_opt.copy()\n            improvement_found = True\n    \n    # If no improvement, use the parent's optimization plan\n    if not improvement_found:\n        seed_for_backup = seed\n        candidate = random_init(420, seed_for_backup)\n        best_candidate = candidate.copy()\n        best_score = evaluate_sequence(candidate)\n    \n    # Final validation and polishing\n    if best_candidate is None:\n        best_candidate = np.ones(420)*0.01\n        best_score = evaluate_sequence(best_candidate)\n    else:\n        # Weights and factors to ensure best_candidate remains bounded and non-negative\n        n = len(best_candidate)\n        size_up = 1000**2\n        sum_val = sum(best_candidate)\n        if sum_val == 0:\n            best_candidate = np.ones(n)*0.01\n            \n        new_best = normalize(best_candidate)\n        # Re-check normalized values using evaluate_sequence to ensure no negative/NaN\n        obj_new = evaluate_sequence(new_best)\n        if obj_new < best_score:\n            best_candidate = new_best\n            best_score = obj_new\n    \n    # All candidate series are valid (non-negative and finite) as of evaluate_sequence checks\n    result_sequence = best_candidate.tolist()\n    return result_sequence\n```\n\nWe'll have to define our helper functions appropriately and ensure the time budget is respected.\n\nThe overall plan is more domain-specific, involved in current research and collaborations.\n\nFinally, to complete our implementation, we wrap up the code structure according to the problem's guidelines.",
